WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:02.534
-Our next guest is
a television host

00:00:02.534 --> 00:00:04.534
and number-one "New York Times"
best-selling author.

00:00:04.534 --> 00:00:07.133
His latest book, "The Lightning
Rod," is available now.

00:00:07.133 --> 00:00:09.634
Please welcome back to the show
Brad Meltzer, everybody.

00:00:09.634 --> 00:00:11.701
♪♪
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:11.701 --> 00:00:17.767
♪♪

00:00:17.767 --> 00:00:23.868
♪♪

00:00:23.868 --> 00:00:26.000
Welcome back to the show!
-It is good to be back here.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:28.234
We are back, right?

00:00:28.234 --> 00:00:29.934
-We're back.
-We're wearing clothes.

00:00:29.934 --> 00:00:32.767
-This sounds not too shabby
to be here with you.

00:00:32.767 --> 00:00:34.667
-I didn't know if we were gonna
come out here

00:00:34.667 --> 00:00:36.467
and there was gonna be
no one here. I had no idea.

00:00:36.467 --> 00:00:38.467
And then, I heard screaming,
and I was like, "It's for me."

00:00:38.467 --> 00:00:40.267
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:40.267 --> 00:00:42.634
Everything else was just warming
up to this moment.

00:00:42.634 --> 00:00:45.200
-My whole life, in fact,
right to here.

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:47.000
-This is fantastic.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:50.234
This is a book that brings back
two of your beloved characters.

00:00:50.234 --> 00:00:53.868
Is it exciting, one, for you
to revisit characters that

00:00:53.868 --> 00:00:56.868
you obviously already have a
relationship with as an author?

00:00:56.868 --> 00:00:59.567
And, two, it must be fun
to write a book that just

00:00:59.567 --> 00:01:00.934
hits the ground running
like this one,

00:01:00.934 --> 00:01:02.901
because it opens just with
fantastic action,

00:01:02.901 --> 00:01:04.100
which I think people --

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:05.400
it's one of the things
they turn to you for.

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:08.734
-Well, what's fun is I always
start with my fears,

00:01:08.734 --> 00:01:10.834
because what kind of neurotic
would I be if I didn't?

00:01:10.834 --> 00:01:15.467
And so the scene opens up with
a character

00:01:15.467 --> 00:01:17.267
who hands his car keys
to a valet.

00:01:17.267 --> 00:01:19.734
And the valet takes
those car keys.

00:01:19.734 --> 00:01:22.300
Instead of parking the car,
he grabs the steering wheel,

00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:25.667
hits the little GPS button,
says the magic words "go home,"

00:01:25.667 --> 00:01:28.767
and now the valet is
driving to the man's house.

00:01:28.767 --> 00:01:29.801
He's got his car keys.

00:01:29.801 --> 00:01:31.400
He's got his house keys
on there.

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:33.567
Because he's going to rob him.
This is a robbery.

00:01:33.567 --> 00:01:36.534
And he gets to the man's house,
goes to break in.

00:01:36.534 --> 00:01:38.767
There's another man
waiting for him with a gun.

00:01:38.767 --> 00:01:40.734
And this isn't a robbery at all,
Seth.

00:01:40.734 --> 00:01:42.200
This is a trap.

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:44.100
And the body turns up.

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:46.200
It comes to our hero Zig,
who's a mortician.

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:48.601
And from there,
he finds something on the body

00:01:48.601 --> 00:01:51.267
no one else sees, and it leads
to one of the government's

00:01:51.267 --> 00:01:52.601
most closely guarded secrets.

00:01:52.601 --> 00:01:54.400
Now, I just ruined chapter 1 of
"The Lightning Rod."

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:55.701
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:55.701 --> 00:01:57.667
-It's totally ruined.
-And it's only two chapters.

00:01:57.667 --> 00:01:58.834
-That's the thing.

00:01:58.834 --> 00:02:00.334
And then, in the end,
everyone's dead.

00:02:00.334 --> 00:02:02.467
[ Laughter ]
So here we are.

00:02:02.467 --> 00:02:05.734
-But it must be so fun to come
up with something like that,

00:02:05.734 --> 00:02:08.300
that, I mean, is gripping
even to hear,

00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:09.734
even more so to read.

00:02:09.734 --> 00:02:12.701
As an author, do you feel that
click in your head

00:02:12.701 --> 00:02:14.501
when you come up with an opening
sequence like that?

00:02:14.501 --> 00:02:17.601
-Well, you know, my son,
for this book -- he never --

00:02:17.601 --> 00:02:19.334
my kids don't care about
anything I do, but he's like,

00:02:19.334 --> 00:02:20.567
"I'm reading this one."

00:02:20.567 --> 00:02:23.067
And we're on vacation,
and he reads chapter 1.

00:02:23.067 --> 00:02:24.934
And I'm terrified, right?

00:02:24.934 --> 00:02:27.534
'Cause I've been reviewed in
all these different places.

00:02:27.534 --> 00:02:29.067
They could say I'm good,
I'm bad, whatever.

00:02:29.067 --> 00:02:30.801
But when your kid is
reading the book --

00:02:30.801 --> 00:02:32.868
-Absolutely.
-And I'm sitting there,

00:02:32.868 --> 00:02:36.567
and he looks up and he says,
"Dad, good opening."

00:02:36.567 --> 00:02:39.033
And I'm just like, "I've done
it. I'm done, I'm finished."

00:02:39.033 --> 00:02:41.300
So that's obviously fun,
but everything kind of becomes,

00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:43.667
you know, part of what I use.

00:02:43.667 --> 00:02:45.767
Like, today is
the 11th anniversary

00:02:45.767 --> 00:02:47.167
since my dad died, right?

00:02:47.167 --> 00:02:48.501
My character's a mortician.

00:02:48.501 --> 00:02:51.567
So even at my mother's funeral,
all these things --

00:02:51.567 --> 00:02:53.234
I just love that I brought
the room down

00:02:53.234 --> 00:02:55.267
by mentioning dead parents.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:55.267 --> 00:02:58.234
But even at my mother's funeral,
you know, they take us in.

00:02:58.234 --> 00:03:01.167
It's this solemn moment
before everybody comes in

00:03:01.167 --> 00:03:03.601
and we're there, and they say,
"We want you to be alone

00:03:03.601 --> 00:03:05.501
with the casket
so you have a moment alone."

00:03:05.501 --> 00:03:08.968
It's my sister. It's myself.
It's my dad.

00:03:08.968 --> 00:03:11.267
It's my -- Two uncles are there.

00:03:11.267 --> 00:03:12.601
No one else is there.

00:03:12.601 --> 00:03:14.634
And they say to us, "You know,

00:03:14.634 --> 00:03:16.133
if you want to say your
goodbyes, you can say them."

00:03:16.133 --> 00:03:18.767
And my dad goes over to
the casket and he leans down

00:03:18.767 --> 00:03:20.834
and kisses the casket over here.

00:03:20.834 --> 00:03:22.067
And then the rabbi says,

00:03:22.067 --> 00:03:23.434
"Actually, her head's
over here,"

00:03:23.434 --> 00:03:25.767
and my uncle says, "Ha-ha,
you finally kissed her ass!"

00:03:25.767 --> 00:03:26.801
[ Laughter ]

00:03:26.801 --> 00:03:28.467
And I'm like,
"This is my family."

00:03:28.467 --> 00:03:29.767
But that's what goes
in the book.

00:03:29.767 --> 00:03:31.801
-It goes in the book.
-Everything's part of the books,

00:03:31.801 --> 00:03:33.734
and the mortician and the death,
it all goes in.

00:03:33.734 --> 00:03:35.901
-You don't just have, obviously,
life experiences.

00:03:35.901 --> 00:03:38.000
You are known for
the level of research

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:41.400
you do for books like these.

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:44.234
And I know over the years,
you've gone in parts of

00:03:44.234 --> 00:03:46.968
government buildings people
like us don't even know exist.

00:03:46.968 --> 00:03:48.267
-Yeah, well, I've done
the secret tunnels

00:03:48.267 --> 00:03:49.434
below the White House.

00:03:49.434 --> 00:03:51.400
I've done the catacombs
below the Capitol,

00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:53.601
and I love --
When I'm reading a thriller,

00:03:53.601 --> 00:03:54.868
I love when I'm learning
something.

00:03:54.868 --> 00:03:56.934
I love that detail.
So for this book, I found out --

00:03:56.934 --> 00:03:58.901
And this is true.
The book's obviously fiction,

00:03:58.901 --> 00:04:02.400
but I found out that
the government has a dozen

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:05.367
secret warehouses hidden
all across the country

00:04:05.367 --> 00:04:07.300
to deal with
bioterrorist attacks.

00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:09.467
So if anthrax or smallpox --

00:04:09.467 --> 00:04:12.901
And the idea is that if there's
an attack in New York,

00:04:12.901 --> 00:04:15.133
there's one in L.A.,
Texas, Idaho,

00:04:15.133 --> 00:04:18.200
wherever it is, there's
a warehouse hidden by an airport

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:20.400
so that they can get an antidote
right to you

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:22.000
within a couple hours.

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:24.267
And I'm like, "You're telling me
the government has

00:04:24.267 --> 00:04:26.567
secret warehouses nobody knows
about and no one can go inside?"

00:04:26.567 --> 00:04:27.968
I'm like, "I gotta go inside."

00:04:27.968 --> 00:04:30.634
So when you get to the final
chapters of "The Lightning Rod,"

00:04:30.634 --> 00:04:33.100
you're not just -- It takes
place in these warehouses.

00:04:33.100 --> 00:04:35.167
Everything you see in there,
I didn't make up.

00:04:35.167 --> 00:04:36.267
It's all real.

00:04:36.267 --> 00:04:37.734
They have cobra venom in there,
man.

00:04:37.734 --> 00:04:39.501
I don't even know what they're
doing with cobra venom,

00:04:39.501 --> 00:04:41.367
but they got cobra venom. I'm
like, "it's going in the book."

00:04:41.367 --> 00:04:45.067
-Yeah. You have some wonderful
children's books as well

00:04:45.067 --> 00:04:47.133
that I would like to talk about
real quick

00:04:47.133 --> 00:04:49.834
because my kids are such
huge fans of them.

00:04:49.834 --> 00:04:53.501
And these are books that sort of
talk about, you know,

00:04:53.501 --> 00:04:55.567
historical figures,
inspiring figures,

00:04:55.567 --> 00:04:58.067
and tell their stories
from when they were kids.

00:04:58.067 --> 00:05:01.200
And you do it with a wonderful
illustrator, Chris Eliopoulos.

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:03.033
-Eliopoulos. Very happy.

00:05:03.033 --> 00:05:05.734
-And how many of these have you
done, and what gave you guys

00:05:05.734 --> 00:05:08.133
this idea to tell stories about
people like Martin Luther King

00:05:08.133 --> 00:05:10.534
and Rosa Parks and
Neil Armstrong and Jim Henson?

00:05:10.534 --> 00:05:13.133
-Yeah, so, you know, we started
this because we were tired of

00:05:13.133 --> 00:05:15.067
our kids being fed garbage
on Instagram.

00:05:15.067 --> 00:05:17.968
I mean, our kids scroll through
every day, and I was like,

00:05:17.968 --> 00:05:19.534
"Let me give you better heroes
to look up to."

00:05:19.534 --> 00:05:21.734
So we did "I Am Amelia Earhart"
and "I Am Abraham Lincoln."

00:05:21.734 --> 00:05:24.667
Our newest ones are "I Am
Muhammad Ali" and "I Am Malala."

00:05:24.667 --> 00:05:27.467
And what happened was is
I saw Muslims being picked on

00:05:27.467 --> 00:05:28.601
and targeted even more so

00:05:28.601 --> 00:05:30.033
than they have been
throughout history.

00:05:30.033 --> 00:05:31.701
And I was like,
"I'm gonna do Muhammad Ali,

00:05:31.701 --> 00:05:34.434
I'm gonna do Malala, and I'm
gonna make sure people learn

00:05:34.434 --> 00:05:36.000
and show them
how to fight back."

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:38.300
Right?
And it was vital to do those.

00:05:38.300 --> 00:05:40.400
-And they're really --
I should stress, you know,

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:42.567
they're beautifully written
books.

00:05:42.567 --> 00:05:45.567
They don't shy away from issues,

00:05:45.567 --> 00:05:48.133
but they're also incredibly
palatable, and I find it

00:05:48.133 --> 00:05:51.767
a great way to introduce ideas
to my kids that way.

00:05:51.767 --> 00:05:54.968
And some of them were taken off
bookshelves for a brief period.

00:05:54.968 --> 00:05:57.501
-They were. It's great.
I mean, they literally banned

00:05:57.501 --> 00:06:00.901
our books "I Am Rosa Parks" and
"I Am Martin Luther King Jr."

00:06:00.901 --> 00:06:02.434
And I'm like -- They put me --

00:06:02.434 --> 00:06:06.234
I got a call from Fox News,
from CNN, from MSNBC.

00:06:06.234 --> 00:06:07.367
They all wanted to have me on.

00:06:07.367 --> 00:06:08.400
They were like,
"This is a travesty."

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:10.801
When Fox News
and CNN and MSNBC agree,

00:06:10.801 --> 00:06:12.234
you know you screwed up.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:06:12.234 --> 00:06:13.667
-Right?
You know you went too far.

00:06:13.667 --> 00:06:16.200
And we fought really hard

00:06:16.200 --> 00:06:18.033
against the school board
in Pennsylvania.

00:06:18.033 --> 00:06:20.100
The students in the area
and the activists in the area,

00:06:20.100 --> 00:06:21.400
we spoke at the school board
meeting.

00:06:21.400 --> 00:06:22.767
We got the ban overturned.

00:06:22.767 --> 00:06:26.601
We told everyone, "If you want
to see this overturned,

00:06:26.601 --> 00:06:28.934
you have to take the lessons of
Doctor King and Rosa Parks.

00:06:28.934 --> 00:06:31.267
You must fight back."
And we did.

00:06:31.267 --> 00:06:32.634
We got it overturned.
But it's happening --

00:06:32.634 --> 00:06:33.868
[ Applause ]
I appreciate the applause.

00:06:33.868 --> 00:06:35.834
I'll take it.

00:06:35.834 --> 00:06:38.234
But the reality is, is
the reason we're seeing this

00:06:38.234 --> 00:06:40.067
happening over
and over and over again --

00:06:40.067 --> 00:06:42.701
You know, after it happened,
I went through history

00:06:42.701 --> 00:06:45.000
and looked at what was
the first books that we banned

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:46.767
as a culture in the country.

00:06:46.767 --> 00:06:48.567
And it was
Harriet Beecher Stowe's.

00:06:48.567 --> 00:06:51.133
"Uncle Tom's Cabin,"
historians agree,

00:06:51.133 --> 00:06:52.968
is the first one we banned.
And why?

00:06:52.968 --> 00:06:55.167
Because slave owners
didn't want us

00:06:55.167 --> 00:06:58.000
talking about how slavery
was bad for people.

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:01.167
They didn't want abolitionist
ideas to be out there.

00:07:01.167 --> 00:07:04.234
And anytime you see a book
banned, it's because someone's

00:07:04.234 --> 00:07:06.701
worried about losing their way
of life and their power.

00:07:06.701 --> 00:07:08.968
And if you're cheering
while books are being

00:07:08.968 --> 00:07:10.834
pulled off of library shelves,

00:07:10.834 --> 00:07:13.501
you are on the wrong side
of history.

00:07:13.501 --> 00:07:14.968
You're on the wrong side of
history.

00:07:14.968 --> 00:07:17.501
So I love the fact that we've
been fighting back against it.

00:07:17.501 --> 00:07:19.834
-I want to ask, because I think
there are gonna be --

00:07:19.834 --> 00:07:22.601
For anyone who's approaching
the idea of wanting to be

00:07:22.601 --> 00:07:24.934
a professional writer, you have
now written over 40 books,

00:07:24.934 --> 00:07:27.100
you're a "New York Times"
number-one best seller.

00:07:27.100 --> 00:07:29.334
You were rejected.
I think your first book,

00:07:29.334 --> 00:07:31.734
I read somewhere,
was rejected 24 times

00:07:31.734 --> 00:07:33.634
by different publishing houses.

00:07:33.634 --> 00:07:38.167
What was it in you that made you
persevere that long?

00:07:38.167 --> 00:07:40.968
-Listen, I was young and I was
stubborn, and I was like,

00:07:40.968 --> 00:07:42.634
"If they don't like that book,
I'm gonna write another."

00:07:42.634 --> 00:07:44.267
I mean, there were 24 rejection
letters.

00:07:44.267 --> 00:07:45.634
There were only 22 publishers.

00:07:45.634 --> 00:07:47.667
I got 24 rejection letters.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:47.667 --> 00:07:49.367
Which means someone
was writing me twice

00:07:49.367 --> 00:07:51.534
to make sure I got the point,
right?

00:07:51.534 --> 00:07:52.868
But I said, "I'm gonna write
another book.

00:07:52.868 --> 00:07:54.434
I'll write another.
I don't care."

00:07:54.434 --> 00:07:57.067
But the truth is --
and this is my secret, right --

00:07:57.067 --> 00:08:01.133
is that 23rd and 24th rejection,
when I got that call,

00:08:01.133 --> 00:08:02.701
I thought they were gonna
be acceptance letters.

00:08:02.701 --> 00:08:05.167
And I was in debt from college.

00:08:05.167 --> 00:08:06.734
I was in debt from law school.

00:08:06.734 --> 00:08:08.834
My agent said,
"Just wait by your phone.

00:08:08.834 --> 00:08:10.567
I'm gonna call you, and I think
you're gonna be out."

00:08:10.567 --> 00:08:12.000
I think I'm gonna get you
out of debt."

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:14.000
And I picked up the phone
all excited,

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:16.434
and I said, "What's the news?"

00:08:16.434 --> 00:08:18.033
And she said, "Sorry, kiddo."

00:08:18.033 --> 00:08:20.167
And my heart just sank.

00:08:20.167 --> 00:08:23.167
And I will tell you, this is
the 25th anniversary of me

00:08:23.167 --> 00:08:26.501
as a published writer, and every
single day I sit down to write,

00:08:26.501 --> 00:08:27.801
I replay that scene.

00:08:27.801 --> 00:08:29.801
I picture the phone
I was holding in my hand.

00:08:29.801 --> 00:08:31.200
It was clear and see-through
with like --

00:08:31.200 --> 00:08:32.334
You could see the wires.

00:08:32.334 --> 00:08:33.467
That was like high-tech
at the time.

00:08:33.467 --> 00:08:35.767
-That was the hip phone
back in the day.

00:08:35.767 --> 00:08:38.701
-And then, I picture the Formica
desk on my left,

00:08:38.701 --> 00:08:40.334
the swivel lamp
every college kid had.

00:08:40.334 --> 00:08:42.667
I picture the bed
and the box spring on my right.

00:08:42.667 --> 00:08:45.567
This little balcony in this
fire station with three doors.

00:08:45.567 --> 00:08:46.934
I count them in my head,
one, two, three.

00:08:46.934 --> 00:08:49.367
And then I say those words,
"Sorry, kiddo"

00:08:49.367 --> 00:08:51.234
because I never, ever want to be

00:08:51.234 --> 00:08:53.300
anything but thankful
for this life I have.

00:08:53.300 --> 00:08:55.501
I never want to ever be
as hungry as I was

00:08:55.501 --> 00:08:57.067
when I was 24 years old.

00:08:57.067 --> 00:08:59.234
I never, ever want to not
appreciate, and I never want

00:08:59.234 --> 00:09:01.367
to think I made it, because
if I think I made it, I'm done.

00:09:01.367 --> 00:09:05.868
For 25 years, every single day
I sit down to write,

00:09:05.868 --> 00:09:09.167
"Sorry, kiddo. Sorry, kiddo.
Sorry, kiddo."

00:09:09.167 --> 00:09:11.534
It's just what I --
I need it to drive me.

00:09:11.534 --> 00:09:13.834
-Well, it's fantastic that
it works as well as it does.

00:09:13.834 --> 00:09:15.067
Thanks so much for being here,
Brad.

00:09:15.067 --> 00:09:16.734
We really appreciate
all of your writing.

00:09:16.734 --> 00:09:18.133
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:09:18.133 --> 00:09:19.968
"The Lightning Rod,"
available now.

